        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Hopewell culture</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Homeland</span><span class="info-stat-val">River valleys of the Eastern Woodlands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, centred on the Scioto River and Paint Creek valleys around Chillicothe, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio">Ohio</a>; key sites the Hopewell Mound Group, Mound City, the Newark Earthworks, Portsmouth Earthworks and Pinson Mounds</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">Middle Woodland period, c. 100 BCE–500 CE; regional groups such as the Goodall focus (c. 200 BCE–500 CE) and the Armstrong and Wilhelm cultures (c. 1–500 CE)</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">geometric earthworks and burial mounds; carved stone platform pipes; ornaments of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and mica such as the Mica Hand; obsidian from Yellowstone; a trade network from Lake <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>; possible lunar and solar alignments</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">How we know them</span><span class="info-stat-val">excavation of mounds and earthworks since Warren K. Moorehead’s work of 1891–1892; grave goods traced to their sources; surveys of earthwork alignments; regional pottery and pipe styles</span></div>
        </div>
