    <section class="kid-tale" aria-labelledby="kid-tale-title">
      <h2 class="kid-tale-title" id="kid-tale-title">The story of the Hopewell culture</h2>
      <ol class="kid-tale-pages"><li class="kid-tale-np"><p>More than 2,000 years ago, many Native American peoples lived along the rivers of the woodlands of North America.</p><p>They lived in small settlements, where they hunted, gathered wild food and grew plants in gardens.</p></li><li class="kid-tale-np"><p>They were not one people, but they were linked by trade from Lake Ontario to Florida.</p><p>They carried copper, shells and a stone called obsidian from far away, some from as far as Yellowstone.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/copper.jpg" alt="Copper" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Copper <span class="kid-tale-cr">Jonathan Zander; derivative work by Materialscientist, CC BY-SA 3.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>Their craftworkers shaped copper, pearls, shells and sharks' teeth into ornaments.</p><p>One mound held more than 200 stone pipes carved in the shapes of animals and birds.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hopewell-culture-national-historical-park.jpg" alt="Hopewell Culture National Historical Park" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Hopewell Culture National Historical Park <span class="kid-tale-cr">Ɱ, CC BY-SA 4.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>They built huge earthworks, walls of earth in circles, squares and octagons, and buried important people in mounds.</p><p>Many of these earthworks survive today, and some in Ohio are a World Heritage Site.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mississippian-culture.jpg" alt="Mississippian culture" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Mississippian culture <span class="kid-tale-cr">Daderot, Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>About 1,500 years ago the trade ended, and they stopped building mounds.</p><p>But the peoples lived on, and some were ancestors of the Mississippian peoples, who built Cahokia.</p></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Hopewell was not one people but a widespread set of Native American communities, from Lake <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>, linked by trade and shared ceremony between about 100 BCE and 500 CE.</li><li>Living in small farming and foraging settlements, they built huge geometric earthworks and burial mounds, filled with goods made from materials carried hundreds of miles.</li><li>Around 500 CE the exchange ended and mound building stopped, but some Hopewell groups are thought to be ancestral to later peoples, among them the builders of Cahokia.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Grave goods at Mound City include hundreds of pounds of obsidian traceable to Yellowstone, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio">Ohio</a> Hopewell may have made pilgrimages there, a walk of about 2,000 miles (3,200 kilometres) each way.</p></aside>
    </section>

